# Line endings are LF everywhere — in the repository AND in the working tree, on every
# platform.
#
# This is not a style preference. Two things in this repo hash file *bytes*:
#
#   1. `ledgerfab/VENDORED.md` carries a SHA-256 manifest of every vendored file, and
#      `test_vendored_ledgerfab.py` recomputes it to prove the fork surface is still zero.
#   2. `evals/golden/` holds byte-compared snapshots of assembled packs.
#
# Without this file, git checks the same commit out as CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux, so
# those hashes differ by platform and the tests fail on a machine where nothing is wrong.
# That is exactly what happened: CI went red on `export.py` while the identical commit was
# green locally. A cross-platform hash has to be over cross-platform bytes.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git must never touch them.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.webm          binary
*.mp4           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary

# `.env` is never committed (see .gitignore) but mark it anyway, so a stray
# `git add -f` cannot mangle a key that happens to contain a CR.
.env            -text

# Generated and vendored files are excluded from GitHub's language stats and diffs, so the
# repo's language breakdown reflects what was actually written here rather than what was
# copied in. `linguist-vendored` keeps them reviewable; it does not hide them.
backend/ledgerfab/**    linguist-vendored
evals/golden/*.json     linguist-generated
fixtures/spendsort/*.csv linguist-generated
frontend/package-lock.json linguist-generated
